# `shape` names the outline of an object. Docs borrow it for an
# interface, a type, or a wire format (`a Duck-shaped value`, `the same
# shape as the v1 response`), and the reader has to guess which one the
# writer meant. The fix names it, so `Duck-shaped` becomes `implements
# Duck` and `the same shape as the v1 response` becomes `the same fields
# as the v1 response`. The verb (`two pressures shape the design`) and
# the idiom `in good shape` hide the same detail, so the rule reports
# every form but `shaping`, which `traffic shaping` keeps as a term of
# art.
#
# Tensor and array docs use `shape` for the size of each dimension, so
# `shape` after `tensor`, `array`, or `matrix` passes, and so does
# `shape of the tensor`. Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes
# the word before `shape` and narrows the finding back to it. A
# sentence that opens with `Shape` therefore passes. Machine-learning
# docs still trip the rule on `output shapes` and `the same shape`, and
# the transformers docs hit it 109 times, nearly all for tensors.
#
# In the docs of 37 other cloned repositories it hit 272 lines, and 38
# of 40 sampled hits used the word for a type, a format, a structure,
# or an idiom. The other two named a literal outline (`a diamond shaped
# icon`).
expr literal [tensor, tensors, array, arrays, matrix, matrices, ndarray]
expr word [shape, shapes]
expr main [<(!@literal @word!(( )of( )DET( )@literal)), @word>, shaped]
let message "`shape`. Name the interface, type, or format it stands for."
let description "Flags `shape` and `X-shaped` where they stand in for an interface, a type, or a format."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The tensor shape must match the weight matrix."
allows "Check the array shape before the call."
allows "The shape of the tensor must match the weights."
allows "The router applies traffic shaping to egress."
allows "Reshape the array before the call."
allows "The mock implements Duck."
